    "narrative": "For this June 20th slate, **Corbin Carroll** (80.64) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) headline our offensive output, both benefiting immensely from high-floor matchups. **Corbin Carroll** is a mandatory start not only for his 80+ efficiency score against Taj Bradley but for the situational green light; with Bradley\u2019s slow delivery, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s elite sprint speed makes him our primary engine for the scarce stolen base category. Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** draws a prime environment against Walbert Ure\u00f1a; the combination of a favorable park shift and a soft matchup against Ure\u00f1a\u2019s xERA of 4.68 creates a high-upside profile for power production. We are also capitalizing on **Jose Caballero** (75.83), who leverages a massive +18% SP skill boost and a +13% platoon advantage against A. Abbott, making him an essential utility play despite the pending status of the lineup.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Brice Turang** (44.78). While his potential for speed is always tantalizing, the optimizer correctly pivoted away from him due to the matchup against a high-tier arm in Chris Sale. The massive -19% SP skill penalty effectively neutralized the boost he would have received from batting third, pushing him below our target threshold of 50. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** remains a standout \"Strong\" play at 71.48; he is positioned for success against Patrick Corbin, where his +15% SP skill advantage and hitter-friendly park profile align to make him a consistent anchor for our AVG and HR needs. \n\nCaution is advised regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for key starters like **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, and **Travis Bazzana**. While their projections remain solid\u2014particularly **Travis Bazzana**'s +19% SP skill boost against Spencer Arrighetti\u2014these ratings are contingent on them maintaining their projected spots in the batting order. We are monitoring these lineups closely; should **Gabriel Moreno** or **Josh Naylor** be pushed down the order, their efficiency scores will likely compress, though their current profiles suggest they remain safer starts than any of our depth pieces like **Zach McKinstry** or **Kyle Karros**, who are currently facing elite pitching or suboptimal order placement.",
